The FedMed system can also be extended to support different machine learning models.

For example:

FedMed
 │
 ├── Logistic Regression
 ├── Neural Network
 ├── SVM
 ├── Decision Tree
 └── Other Federated Models

For actual FedAvg-style federated learning, the model needs parameters that can be meaningfully aggregated. Neural networks and linear models are commonly suited to this approach.

Different models can also be evaluated using:

Accuracy
Precision
Recall
F1-score
ROC-AUC
Confusion matrix


Computational Scalability

As the number of clients increases, the federated server has to process more model updates.

A simplified process is:

Client 1 ──┐
Client 2 ──┤
Client 3 ──┤
Client 4 ──┼──→ Server
Client 5 ──┤
Client N ──┘
             ↓
       Aggregate Updates
             ↓
        Global Model

To support larger deployments, the system can use:

Multi-core processing
Distributed servers
Cloud infrastructure
Containerization
Load balancing
Asynchronous client participation


Network Scalability

Federated learning requires communication between clients and the federated server.

A typical communication cycle is:

Server
  ↓
Global Model
  ↓
Clients
  ↓
Local Training
  ↓
Model Updates
  ↓
Server
  ↓
Aggregation

As the number of clients increases, communication traffic also increases.

FedMed can reduce communication requirements through techniques such as:

Model compression
Update compression
Fewer communication rounds
Client selection
Quantization
Efficient serialization


 API Scalability

The FedMed prediction API can also be designed to support multiple users.

For example:

User 1 ──┐
User 2 ──┤
User 3 ──┼──→ FedMed API ──→ ML Model
User 4 ──┤
User N ──┘

Instead of processing only one request, the API can eventually support many concurrent requests using appropriate deployment infrastructure.

Possible technologies include:

FastAPI
Uvicorn
Docker
Cloud deployment
Load balancing


Dashboard Scalability

The Streamlit dashboard can provide a user interface for the system.

A future architecture could be:

Healthcare User
       ↓
FedMed Dashboard
       ↓
FedMed API
       ↓
Prediction Model
       ↓
Prediction Result

For larger deployments, the dashboard and API can be deployed separately.

This provides better separation between:

User interface
API
ML model
Federated server
Data processing


Storage Scalability

Medical datasets can become very large.

Instead of storing everything in one local CSV file, a production-oriented FedMed system could use:

Local Hospital Storage
        ↓
Database / Data Lake
        ↓
Local ML Pipeline
        ↓
Federated Learning

Possible storage technologies could include:

PostgreSQL
MySQL
MongoDB
Cloud object storage
Data lakes

For your current M.Tech prototype, CSV files are sufficient for demonstrating the pipeline. Larger deployments would require more scalable storage infrastructure.

Horizontal Scalability

Horizontal scaling means adding more machines or clients rather than making one machine increasingly powerful.

For FedMed:

          Federated Server
                │
       ┌────────┼────────┐
       ↓        ↓        ↓
    Server 1 Server 2 Server 3

This approach can distribute computational workloads across multiple machines.

It is particularly relevant when the number of participating organizations grows.

Vertical Scalability

Vertical scaling means increasing the resources of an existing machine.

For example:

4 GB RAM
   ↓
8 GB RAM
   ↓
16 GB RAM
   ↓
32 GB RAM

Similarly, CPU/GPU resources can be increased to handle larger datasets or more computationally intensive models.

Scalability Through Modular Architecture

FedMed's modular architecture also makes future expansion easier.

FedMed
│
├── Privacy
├── Data Validation
├── Preprocessing
├── Federated Learning
├── Model Training
├── Evaluation
├── Prediction API
├── Dashboard
└── Monitoring

Each component can be improved independently.

For example, you can upgrade the prediction API without completely rewriting the data-validation module.